    "rationale": "The objective (communication_kernel_sum_ms, max-rank NCCL kernel duration sum, median of 3 profiled samples) is produced only by the 13 fp32 c10d::allreduce_ bucket invocations of DDP's final-micro-batch reducer: call d0938de87369efd500322679 (12 buckets of 7,087,872 elements) and call b8ade439d7b8bd5fb5c1029b (the 39,385,344-element last bucket), 497,759,232 B of gradient payload per rank in total. Measured facts from the supplied records: the clean objective (63.32/63.67 ms on ranks 0/1) matches the warmed intrinsic back-to-back collective cost at exactly these sizes (collective_calibration: 12 x 3.536 ms + 19.383 ms), so the metric is transfer-bound, not overlap- or skew-bound; the executed kernel is ncclDevKernel_AllReduce_Sum_f32_RING_LL launched as grid [2,1,1] x block [544,1,1] on the no-P2P two-RTX-4090 PCIe4 topology, i.e. 2 channels; achieved logical throughput is ~8.0-8.1 GB/s per direction, consistent with the tuner's LL rating (8.6 GB/s) and about half of its RING Simple/LL128 ratings (19.8/19.0 GB/s). Bytes, dtypes, bucket layout (bucket_cap_mb=25, gradient_as_bucket_view, broadcast_buffers=False), no_sync accumulation, SGD and the default process group are frozen contract invariants, so bucket fusion, compression, hooks and DDP reconfiguration are excluded. This candidate is the selected joint hypothesis: both allreduce_ sites get a joint functional replacement that performs the identical fp32 SUM bucket allreduce (same tensor list reduced in place, same async semantics, same (Tensor[], Work) return through the real c10d::allreduce_ operator) on a lazily created, identically-membership NCCL process group whose communicator is initialized with raised channel parallelism (NCCL_NCHANNELS) and optionally a pinned protocol (NCCL_PROTO), with the expectation of raising per-kernel SHM ring throughput from ~8 GB/s toward the PCIe ceiling and cutting the kernel-duration sum roughly proportionally (target ~35-45 ms). This expectation is a hypothesis to be measured, not an observed capability; whether channel scaling raises SHM bus bandwidth and whether protocol pinning helps at 28 MB payloads are unknowns the parameter search resolves empirically (nchannels 0/4/8/16 x proto default/Simple/LL128; 0/default arms isolate channel-only, protocol-only and control effects). Numerics: a 2-rank ring/tree SUM reduces each element exactly once from the two replicas and broadcasts the identical bytes, so cross-rank gradients stay bit-identical (exact_gradient_replica_check at atol=0/rtol=0), and the serial-reference checks (1e-6/1e-4) see the same single-addition values as the baseline; parameters, gradients and the optimizer step are untouched. The tuned communicator is created once on the first replacement invocation (first quality/warmup step, per the binding contract's lazy-setup allowance) symmetrically on both ranks via dist.new_group(backend='nccl', device_id=<bucket device>), with env overrides applied only around creation/eager-init and restored after the first successful tuned call; the worker's default-group communicator (already initialized during prepare) and the gloo control group are untouched, and no other NCCL communicator is created in the worker afterwards, so env-based tuning stays confined to the new communicator. Fallbacks keep the parent semantics exactly: unsupported signature (defensive runtime guards beyond the framework signature match), setup failure (communicator creation error; permanent per-process fallback with a concise label) and runtime failure (tuned call raised; per-process fallback) all route the unmodified parent operator with the original arguments, so quality, state and alias semantics never depend on the tuned path. Clean step_ms is expected to be unchanged or better (the reduced kernels lie inside the timed step) and must stay within the 1.05x constraint; the rendezvous and communicator-init cost occur only in the first, un-timed quality invocation. No bundle is replaced or